Conjugacy and posterior derivation
A prior family is conjugate for a likelihood when every posterior remains in that family. Given \(s\) successes among \(n\) trials, \[\begin{aligned}
\pi(\theta\mid s)&\propto \underbrace{\theta^s(1-\theta)^{n-s}}_{\text{likelihood kernel}}
\underbrace{\theta^{a-1}(1-\theta)^{b-1}}_{\text{prior kernel}}\\
&=\theta^{(a+s)-1}(1-\theta)^{(b+n-s)-1}.
\end{aligned}\] Recognizing the beta kernel on \((0,1)\) gives \[\theta\mid s\sim\operatorname{Beta}(A,B),\qquad A=a+s,\ B=b+n-s.\] Thus the normalized posterior is \(\theta^{A-1}(1-\theta)^{B-1}/B(A,B)\) on that interval.
